              "function_response": null,
              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The word **\"comet\"** has two main meanings: its historical, literal meaning from language (etymology) and its modern scientific definition. \n\n### 1. The Literal Meaning (Etymology)\nHistorically, the word **comet** literally means **\"long-haired.\"**\n\n* **Greek Origin:** It comes from the Ancient Greek word ***kom\u0113t\u0113s* (\u03ba\u03bf\u03bc\u03ae\u03c4\u03b7\u03c2)**, which means \"wearing long hair\" or \"long-haired.\" This is derived from ***kom\u0113* (\u03ba\u03cc\u03bc\u03b7)**, meaning \"hair of the head.\"\n* **\"Long-Haired Stars\":** Around 500 BC, ancient Greek philosophers began calling these objects *asteres kom\u0113tes*, which translates to **\"long-haired stars.\"** They chose this name because the glowing, flowing tail of a comet looked like long, streaming hair brushing across the night sky. \n* **Evolution of the Word:** The word transitioned from Greek into Latin (*cometa*), then into Old French (*comete*), and finally into Middle English around the year 1200.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Scientific Definition\nIn modern astronomy, a **comet** is an icy, small Solar System body that orbits the Sun. \n\nComets are essentially \"dirty snowballs\"\u2014mixtures of frozen gases, water ice, dust, and rocky debris left over from the formation of the solar system about 4.6 billion years ago. They are characterized by three distinct stages or features:\n* **The Nucleus:** The solid, frozen core of the comet.\n* **The Coma:** When a comet's orbit brings it close to the Sun, the heat vaporizes the ice. This process (called *outgassing*) releases gases and dust, creating a giant, glowing atmosphere around the nucleus called a *coma*.\n* **The Tail:** As the comet gets closer to the Sun, solar wind and radiation push the dust and gas away from the comet, forming the famous, glowing tail (which always points away from the Sun).\n\n### Summary\nWhile a scientist defines a comet as an **icy celestial body that outgasses as it nears the Sun**, the word itself is a poetic description from the ancient world, meaning **\"long-haired star.\"**",
